Found while running lighting-match-engine-core through an open-source matching-engine benchmark, the [Matching Engine Performance Challenge](https://github.com/flash1-dev/matching-engine-benchmark) — it cross-checks engines against the byte-identical consensus of Liquibook, QuantCup and Exchange-core.

On `SparseOrderBook` (`src/orderbook/sparse.rs`, `main` @ `381aeda`), `prune_bucket_front` (sparse.rs ~8–16) erases by id, not by instance:

```rust
fn prune_bucket_front(
    bucket: &mut OrdersBucket,
    order_map: &mut AHashMap<u64, (bool, u64)>,
) {
    while matches!(bucket.orders.front(), Some(order) if !order.is_active()) {
        let removed = bucket.orders.pop_front().unwrap();
        order_map.remove(&removed.order_id);   // <-- erases by id, not by instance
    }
}
```

**Cause.** Every order is already pulled from `order_map` when it goes inactive — `cancel_order` at sparse.rs:70, fully-filled orders inline at sparse.rs:222 — so this `remove` is normally a harmless no-op. Not in the cancel-then-reinsert-same-id case: after the live order re-adds its id to `order_map`, pruning the dead duplicate at the front removes that id again, deleting the **live** order's index entry. The order keeps resting and matching, but the engine can no longer find it by id, so a later `cancel_order` falls into `None => return false` and spuriously fails.

**Repro** Submit X; cancel X while it's not the front (tombstone left behind); reinsert under the same id X at the same price; trigger a front-prune via the next match — `cancel_order(X)` now returns `false` even though X is resting.

**Fix.** Ownership is already handled when each order goes inactive, so the prune should just drop dead front orders and leave the index alone:

```rust
fn prune_bucket_front(bucket: &mut OrdersBucket, _order_map: &mut AHashMap<u64, (bool, u64)>) {
    while matches!(bucket.orders.front(), Some(order) if !order.is_active()) {
        bucket.orders.pop_front();
    }
}
```

Now `order_map` holds exactly the live resting set and a reinserted-same-id order isn't accidentally evicted.

