Found while running Orderbook through an open-source matching-engine benchmark, the [Matching Engine Performance Challenge](https://github.com/flash1-dev/matching-engine-benchmark) — it cross-checks engines against the byte-identical consensus of other open source engines. (Separate from the `FillAndKill` deadlock I reported earlier; this one is at teardown.)

Destroying an `Orderbook` can block indefinitely. The book matches correctly throughout — it only hangs *after* the work is done, in the destructor.

`Orderbook::Orderbook()` starts a background "prune GoodForDay orders" thread (`Orderbook.cpp:264`, current `master` `dd136dd`). That thread sleeps on a condition variable until 16:00 local time (`PruneGoodForDayOrders`, `:7`):

```cpp
auto till = next - now + milliseconds(100);          // :27  — ~time until next 16:00
{
    std::unique_lock ordersLock{ ordersMutex_ };     // :30
    if (shutdown_.load(std::memory_order_acquire) ||
        shutdownConditionVariable_.wait_for(ordersLock, till) == std::cv_status::no_timeout)
        return;
}
```

`~Orderbook()` asks it to stop and joins it (`:266`):

```cpp
shutdown_.store(true, std::memory_order_release);    // :268
shutdownConditionVariable_.notify_one();             // :269
ordersPruneThread_.join();                            // :270
```

**Cause — lost wakeup.** The destructor publishes `shutdown_` and calls `notify_one()` **without holding `ordersMutex_`** (the mutex the prune thread holds across its check-and-wait). `notify_one()` only wakes a thread already blocked in `wait_for`. If it runs in the window after the prune thread has read `shutdown_ == false` (`:32`) but before it is registered as a waiter inside `wait_for` (`:33`), the notification is lost. `wait_for` then blocks for the full `till` — until 16:00 — and `ordersPruneThread_.join()` blocks with it, so the destructor (and the thread destroying the book) hangs for up to ~24 hours.

It is a race, so it hides in single-threaded smoke tests: construction and the first `wait_for` are microseconds apart, the destructor usually runs much later, and the notify lands cleanly. Under scheduling pressure it surfaces — in the benchmark harness, running many short-lived `Orderbook` instances under parallel load, **~7% of instances hung at teardown; zero in isolated/sequential runs**. A debugger on a hung process shows the prune thread parked in `pthread_cond_clockwait` (still waiting until 16:00) and the main thread parked in `pthread_join`.

**Fix.** The minimal change — publishing `shutdown_` under `ordersMutex_` before notifying — narrows the window but, in my testing, does not fully close it (and relocating the synchronization onto the book mutex risks contending the matcher). The robust fix is to take the condition variable out of the shutdown path entirely and have the prune thread poll the atomic flag in bounded slices, so it never registers as a cv waiter — teardown can then neither lose a wakeup nor race the cv's own destructor:

```cpp
// replaces the unique_lock / wait_for block above
bool deadline_reached = false;
while (!shutdown_.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(till < milliseconds(100) ? till : milliseconds(100));
    if (system_clock::now() >= next) { deadline_reached = true; break; }
}
if (!deadline_reached)
    return;   // shutdown requested
// else: 16:00 reached — fall through to the existing GoodForDay prune
```

This keeps the 16:00 GoodForDay semantics (it still falls through to prune once `next` elapses), touches no shared lock so it adds no matcher contention, and needs `#include <thread>`. With it, teardown is bounded to one poll interval. Verified against the benchmark: **0 hangs in 160 runs under 44-way parallel load** (vs ~7% unpatched), and matching output is unchanged — byte-identical trade-report hash on all five scenarios.

Happy to share the failing workload.

Found while running this matching engine through an open-source matching-engine benchmark, the [Matching Engine Performance Challenge](https://github.com/flash1-dev/matching-engine-benchmark) — it cross-checks engines against the byte-identical consensus of Liquibook, QuantCup and Exchange-core.

When an incoming order crosses and fills, the book mutates and `matchedQuantity_` grows, but no trade ever reaches the market-data feed. `MDAdapter::notify_trade(...)` is declared and wired all the way to `MarketDataPublisher::publish_trade(...)` (`MDAdapter.h:29`, `MarketDataPublisher.cpp:36`), yet nothing calls it. A consumer subscribed to the feed sees price-level updates but no executions — no print, no executed price/quantity, no aggressor side.

**Cause.** `OrderBook<MarketDataPublisher>::TryMatch` (starts at `OrderBook.cpp:298` on `main` @ `b5984aac`) is the only place fills happen. Per resting order it decrements quantities (`OrderBook.cpp:320-321`), bumps `matchedQuantity_` (line 330), and publishes the resulting size change via `md_adapter_.notify_price_level_change(...)` (line 322) — but never `md_adapter_.notify_trade(...)`. A grep of the tree finds `notify_trade` exactly once: its definition at `MDAdapter.h:29`. Zero callers. So the entire publication path downstream of the hook is complete and simply never reached.

```cpp
// OrderBook.cpp:320-323  (inside the per-resting-order fill loop)
opposingOrderPtr->DecreaseQuantity(matchedQty);
limit->DecreaseQuantity(matchedQty);
md_adapter_.notify_price_level_change(opposingPrice, restingQty - matchedQty, restingQty,
    !isBuy); // TODO: This should happen within the limit
// ... matchedQuantity_ += matchedQty;  (line 330)
// ... no md_adapter_.notify_trade(...) anywhere in this function
```

**Repro** Submit a resting limit, then a crossing order on the opposite side: the book updates and `matchedQuantity_` increases, but `publish_trade` is never invoked on the configured `MarketDataPublisher`. A mock that asserts `publish_trade` fires on a crossing fill shows it directly.

**Fix.** Emit the trade at the fill site, next to the existing level-change call. `OrderBook` has no trade-id counter today (private members are only `instrument_`, `matchedQuantity_`, `md_adapter_`, `askLimits_`, `bidLimits_`, `orders_`), so add one:

```cpp
// in class OrderBook's private section (OrderBook.h)
uint64_t nextTradeId_ = 1;
```

then call the hook:

```cpp
opposingOrderPtr->DecreaseQuantity(matchedQty);
limit->DecreaseQuantity(matchedQty);
md_adapter_.notify_price_level_change(opposingPrice, restingQty - matchedQty, restingQty, !isBuy);
md_adapter_.notify_trade(/*trade_id=*/nextTradeId_++, /*price=*/opposingPrice,
                         /*quantity=*/matchedQty, /*buyerAggressed=*/isBuy);
```

Worth widening `notify_trade`'s signature too: it currently carries only an aggressor boolean (`notify_trade(trade_id, price, quantity, buyerAggressed)`), so even once it fires a consumer can't tell *which two orders* traded. Passing the resting/maker and incoming/taker ids would let executions be attributed.

(The trade-count / book-state divergence I saw under load turned out to be a separate cancel-path bug — a double-unlink in `RemoveOrder` — which I'm filing as its own report.)

