Found while running lighting-match-engine-core through an open-source matching-engine benchmark, the [Matching Engine Performance Challenge](https://github.com/flash1-dev/matching-engine-benchmark) — it cross-checks engines against the byte-identical consensus of Liquibook, QuantCup and Exchange-core.

`SparseOrderBook` (`src/orderbook/sparse.rs`, `main` @ `381aeda`) prunes inactive (cancelled / fully-filled) orders only lazily, at the *front* of a bucket. `execute_matching` (sparse.rs ~190–224) then walks the bucket with `front_mut()` and emits a `Trade` without ever re-checking `is_active()`:

```rust
while taker.remaining_quantity > 0 && !bucket.orders.is_empty() {
    let resting = bucket.orders.front_mut().unwrap();
    let qty = taker.remaining_quantity.min(resting.remaining_quantity);

    taker.remaining_quantity -= qty;
    resting.remaining_quantity -= qty;
    // ...
    outcome.add_trade(Trade {
        // ...
        quantity: qty,          // <-- emitted even when qty == 0
        // ...
    });

    if resting.remaining_quantity == 0 {
        let removed = bucket.orders.pop_front().unwrap();
        order_map.remove(&removed.order_id);
    }
}
```

**Cause.** `match_buy`/`match_sell` call `prune_bucket_front` exactly once before entering the loop (sparse.rs:127 / :156), but the loop never prunes again. A cancel sets `remaining_quantity = 0` and `is_cancelled = true` yet only pops the front when the cancelled order *is* the front at cancel time (the `if is_front` branch, sparse.rs:93) — so a cancelled order resting behind a live one stays in the queue. Once the live orders ahead of it are consumed inside the loop, the tombstone surfaces as the new front and the loop computes `qty = taker.remaining_quantity.min(0) = 0`, pushing a zero-quantity `Trade` before popping it. The dense book sidesteps this by re-pruning the front on *every* `while` iteration (dense.rs:177 / :221), so it always matches an active order.

**Repro** Rest sells A then B at the same price (`add_resting_order` pushes to the back, so the queue is `[A, B]`); cancel **B**, the non-front order, so it becomes a tombstone behind live A; send an aggressor big enough to fully consume A. A pops, B surfaces, and the loop emits a `quantity: 0` trade. In the benchmark (10⁶-message U.S.-equity workload, ~95% cancels, byte-level verification) the un-patched engine printed **73,363** Trade reports vs the consensus's **62,474** on the `normal` scenario — **10,889 extra, every one zero-quantity** against a cancelled front order, first diverging at sequence 1,499 (a 0-share print against cancelled order 503555).

**Fix.** Prune the front before reading it each iteration, like the dense book:

```rust
while taker.remaining_quantity > 0 {
    Self::prune_bucket_front(bucket, order_map);
    let Some(resting) = bucket.orders.front_mut() else { break };
    let qty = taker.remaining_quantity.min(resting.remaining_quantity);
    // ... unchanged ...
}
```

With the front always active, `qty` can no longer be 0.

