Found while integrating `zhaocong6/match` into an open matching-engine benchmark, the [Matching Engine Performance Challenge](https://github.com/flash1-dev/matching-engine-benchmark) — it cross-checks engines against the byte-identical consensus of other open-source engines. The book lost depth on cancels, and it traced back to `buySellQueue.del` removing the whole price-level node when only one order at that level was being cancelled.

Pinned at current `master` (`ddeb860`); built with Go 1.23.4 (the module declares `go 1.13`; the defect is in the price-level bookkeeping and is independent of the Go version), deps `github.com/google/btree v1.0.0` + `github.com/shopspring/decimal`. The standalone test below reproduces it via `go test -run TestDelOrphansPriceLevel`.

When two or more orders rest at the same price on one side, cancelling any single one of them removes the entire price level from the book — every other order at that price silently disappears. A cancel of order A also drops the untouched orders B, C, … that share A's price, so resting depth is lost and a later aggressor that should have matched them finds nothing.

**Repro.** Drop this in the package as `del_repro_test.go` and run `go test -run TestDelOrphansPriceLevel ./...`:

```go
package match

import (
	"testing"

	"github.com/shopspring/decimal"
)

// Two distinct resting buys at the SAME price; cancelling one must leave the other.
func TestDelOrphansPriceLevel(t *testing.T) {
	q := newBuyQueue()
	p := decimal.NewFromInt(100)
	q.push(&Order{ID: "A", Price: p, Amount: decimal.NewFromInt(5), Side: BUY, Type: LIMIT, Time: 1})
	q.push(&Order{ID: "B", Price: p, Amount: decimal.NewFromInt(7), Side: BUY, Type: LIMIT, Time: 2})

	q.del(&Order{Price: p, Side: BUY, Type: LIMIT, Time: 1}) // cancel A only

	got := q.getDepth(10)
	if len(got) != 1 {
		t.Fatalf("after cancelling A, expected 1 resting order (B), got %d: %+v", len(got), got)
	}
}
```

Actual:

```
--- FAIL: TestDelOrphansPriceLevel
    del_repro_test.go: after cancelling A, expected 1 resting order (B), got 0: []
```

B was dropped even though only A was cancelled. The same path is reached from the public API: `matcher.Cancel` → `dispatch.cancel` → `exec.cancel` (the `LIMIT` arm) → `book.del` → `buySellQueue.del`.

**Mechanism / root cause.** `buySellQueue.del` (`match_struct.go:205-226`):

```go
func (q *buySellQueue) del(order *Order) *Order {
	q.Lock()
	defer q.Unlock()

	i := q.tree.Delete(&priceTree{       // (1) removes the WHOLE price level
		price: order.Price,
	})

	if tree, ok := i.(*priceTree); ok {
		t := tree.queue.del(order)        // (2) deletes one order from that level's time queue

		// 验证价格tree是否长度为0
		// 删除长度为0的价格tree
		if tree.queue.len() == 0 {
			q.tree.Delete(i)              // (3) no-op: i was already removed at (1)
		}

		return t
	}

	return nil
}
```

Step (1) detaches the entire `priceTree` node (which owns the time queue holding *all* orders at that price) from the price btree. Step (2) deletes just the single requested order from that detached queue. If the queue still holds other orders (`len() > 0`), nothing puts the level back — the `if` at step (3) only fires when the queue is now empty, and even then it re-deletes an already-removed node. So every order that shared the cancelled order's price is orphaned along with the level.

For contrast, `buyQueue.pop` / `sellQueue.pop` (`:263-281`, `:357-375`) handle the same situation correctly — they take the level via `Max()` / `Min()` and only `q.tree.Delete(i)` when `tree.queue.len() == 0`, leaving a non-empty level in place. `del` is the one path that removes the level unconditionally up front.

**Fix.** Fetch the level with `Get` instead of `Delete`; the existing empty-check then removes it only when the last order is gone. One line:

```diff
 func (q *buySellQueue) del(order *Order) *Order {
 	q.Lock()
 	defer q.Unlock()

-	i := q.tree.Delete(&priceTree{
+	i := q.tree.Get(&priceTree{
 		price: order.Price,
 	})

 	if tree, ok := i.(*priceTree); ok {
 		t := tree.queue.del(order)

 		// 验证价格tree是否长度为0
 		// 删除长度为0的价格tree
 		if tree.queue.len() == 0 {
 			q.tree.Delete(i)
 		}

 		return t
 	}

 	return nil
 }
```

With this change the repro above passes (B survives), and a cancel that empties a level still removes the level via the existing `len() == 0` branch. This mirrors the conditional-delete pattern already used by `pop`.

