Hi, I'm using DIE library in a Qt/C++ application and looking for more efficient approaches to handle scanning scenarios, particularly:
- Large executable files (>50MB) that take considerable time to scan
- Multiple files in batch processing where sequential scanning becomes very slow

Here's my current implementation:
```cpp
char* result = DIE_ScanFileA(byteArray.data(), DIE_ALLTYPESSCAN | DIE_HEURISTICSCAN | DIE_VERBOSE | DIE_RESULTASJSON, dbByteArray.data());
if (!result) {
    qDebug() << "Scan failed! Check database path: " << dbPath;
} else {
    qDebug() << result;
    results << parseDieResult(result);
    DIE_FreeMemoryA(result);
}
```

Performance Challenges:
- Single large files cause significant processing delays
- Scanning multiple files sequentially has linear time accumulation

Looking for guidance on:
- Recommended patterns for batch processing multiple files efficiently
- Optimization strategies for large executable scanning
- Asynchronous or parallel scanning capabilities
- Appropriate scan flags to balance speed and detection quality
