Found while running MatchingEngine through an open-source matching-engine benchmark, the [Matching Engine Performance Challenge](https://github.com/flash1-dev/matching-engine-benchmark) — it cross-checks engines against the byte-identical consensus of other open source engines.

An aggressor trades against the **oldest crossing order in the book, not the best-priced one**. With more than one crossable level resting, that fills the wrong counterparty at a worse price, and every fill, cancel and modify after it drifts because the book evolved against the wrong makers.

**Cause.** Each side of the book is a plain `std::list<COrder>` (`source/engine.h:37-38`) and new resting orders are always appended at `end()` — a passive sell goes to `m_sellbook.insert(m_sellbook.end(), ...)` (`source/engine.cpp:59`) and a passive buy to `m_buybook.insert(m_buybook.end(), ...)` (`source/engine.cpp:115`). So the list is in pure insertion (time) order. `Match()` then scans from `begin()` (`source/engine.cpp:12-13` and `:67-68`) and trades against the **first order that merely crosses**:

```cpp
// sell hitting the bid book (source/engine.cpp:14-17)
for (; it != m_buybook.end();)
{
    if (order.GetPrice() <= it->GetPrice())   // any crossing bid, oldest first
    {
        ...
```

```cpp
// buy hitting the ask book (source/engine.cpp:69-72)
for (; it != m_sellbook.end();)
{
    if (order.GetPrice() >= it->GetPrice())   // any crossing ask, oldest first
    {
        ...
```

There is no best-price selection anywhere in the two match loops (`source/engine.cpp:14-54` for a sell into the bid book, `:69-110` for a buy into the ask book). The list is never kept price-sorted: `SortBook` (`source/engine.cpp:184`) only sorts a throwaway copy for `Print()` output and never reorders the live book. So the first level the scan reaches that satisfies `<=` / `>=` wins, regardless of whether a better-priced level is resting further down.

This stays hidden as long as a static book never holds two crossable levels in the wrong time order, which is why the benchmark's constant-mid `static` scenario passed clean while all four moving scenarios diverged.

**Repro.** Rest two bids — `BUY GFD 100 10 A` first, then `BUY GFD 105 10 B`. B@105 is now the best bid, A@100 the oldest. Send `SELL GFD 100 10 S`. Price-time priority must fill **B@105** (best bid, better price for the seller). This engine scans `m_buybook` from `begin()`, hits `A@100` first, finds `100 <= 100` true at `source/engine.cpp:17`, and trades the seller against **A@100** — older, worse-priced, wrong counterparty — leaving B@105 resting. The consensus engines print one trade at 105 and leave A untouched.

**Fix.** Select the best-priced crossing level instead of the first one. Either keep each book price-sorted on insert (sort the bid book descending by price and the ask book ascending, ties by time, so `begin()` is always the best level — then the existing `begin()`-forward scan is already correct), or, before matching, walk the side and pick the max-price bid / min-price ask among the crossing orders and trade against that. With the makers chosen by price-then-time, the book evolves the same way the consensus engines do and the downstream trade/cancel/modify counts line up.

Happy to share the failing workload.

Found while running oceanbook through an open-source matching-engine benchmark, the [Matching Engine Performance Challenge](https://github.com/flash1-dev/matching-engine-benchmark) — it cross-checks engines against the byte-identical consensus of other open source engines. The matcher itself is clean: oceanbook's trade report reproduces the consensus byte-for-byte on all five scenarios. The break is in the depth / market-data subsystem next to it.

Both depth-update call sites put the *quantity* into `PriceLevel.Price` and never set `PriceLevel.Quantity`. In `insertOrder` (`pkg/orderbook/orderbook.go`, current `master` `a7768ee`):

```go
// reduce a maker on a fill (orderbook.go:125)
od.depth.UpdatePriceLevel(&PriceLevel{
    Side:  bestOrder.Side,
    Price: newTrade.Quantity.Neg(),   // <-- fill quantity goes into Price
})

// rest the remainder (orderbook.go:148)
od.depth.UpdatePriceLevel(&PriceLevel{
    Side:  newOrder.Side,
    Price: newOrder.PendingQuantity(), // <-- resting quantity goes into Price
})
```

**Cause.** `Depth.UpdatePriceLevel` (`pkg/orderbook/depth.go:82`) keys levels by `PriceLevel.Key()`, which is `{Price, Side}` (`depth.go:35`), and aggregates `pl.Quantity` (`depth.go:103`). Since `Quantity` is left at its zero value at both call sites, every update keys the level by the quantity value and contributes zero size. So a real order at price 100 for 10 lots creates a depth level at *price 10* with `Quantity = 0`, and a 4-lot fill against it creates another level at *price -4* with `Quantity = 0`. The actual price and the resting quantity are never recorded anywhere. `Count` is also never set, so the pruning guard `existedPriceLevel.Count == 0 || ... Quantity ... Equal(Zero)` (`depth.go:106`) keeps firing on these zero-quantity levels.

This `Depth` is the public market-data surface: `Service.GetDepth` returns `od.GetDepth().Serialize()` over gRPC (`pkg/service/oceanbook/service.go:52`). So every depth response a client receives carries bogus prices and zero quantities.

It does not touch matching, which is why the trade report is byte-identical to the consensus: the matcher reads the bid/ask red-black trees (`insertOrder` walks `makerBooks.Right()`), never `Depth`. `Depth` is a write-only side structure that only the market-data path reads.

**Repro.** Driving the engine directly:

```go
ob := NewOrderBook("REPRO")
// rest ask id=1 price=100 qty=10
ob.InsertOrder(&order.Order{ID: 1, Side: order.SideAsk,
    Price: decimal.New(100, 0), Quantity: decimal.New(10, 0)})
// partial-fill with bid id=2 price=100 qty=4
ob.InsertOrder(&order.Order{ID: 2, Side: order.SideBid,
    Price: decimal.New(100, 0), Quantity: decimal.New(4, 0)})

for _, v := range ob.GetDepth().Asks.Values() {
    pl := v.(*PriceLevel)
    fmt.Printf("price=%s qty=%s count=%d\n", pl.Price, pl.Quantity, pl.Count)
}
```

The trade is correct — `price=100 qty=4 maker=1 taker=2`. But the ask depth prints

```
price=10 qty=0 count=0
price=-4 qty=0 count=0
```

instead of the one level a correct book must show, `price=100 qty=6 count=1`.

**Fix.** Set `Price` to the order's price and `Quantity` to the resting / Δ quantity, and maintain `Count`, at both sites:

```go
// reduce a maker on a fill (orderbook.go:125)
od.depth.UpdatePriceLevel(&PriceLevel{
    Side:     bestOrder.Side,
    Price:    bestOrder.Price,
    Quantity: newTrade.Quantity.Neg(),
    // decrement Count when bestOrder.Filled() leaves the book
})

// rest the remainder (orderbook.go:148)
od.depth.UpdatePriceLevel(&PriceLevel{
    Side:     newOrder.Side,
    Price:    newOrder.Price,
    Quantity: newOrder.PendingQuantity(),
    Count:    1,
})
```

The `Count` is worth fixing alongside the swap: with `Count` left at zero, the `Count == 0` half of the pruning guard at `depth.go:106` deletes the level the moment a fill touches it, so even after the Price/Quantity swap the level disappears. With both fixed, the repro above reports `price=100 qty=6 count=1` as expected, and matching is unaffected (the matcher never reads `Depth`).

